TI  - Can Crowdsourcing Survive the LLM Era? A Community Survey on Human Data Collection
AB  - The widespread use of large language models (LLMs) as writing tools challenges the validity of crowdsourced data, as crowdworkers may 'outsource' tasks to models.To better understand how the research community addresses this, we surveyed 155 researchers in NLP and related disciplines about their experiences and opinions on collecting free-text responses via crowdsourcing in the LLM era.This paper provides an overview of practitioners' challenges, mitigation strategies, and the foreseen implications on data quality.44% of respondents reported observing LLM usage in their crowdsourced data.While 93.2% of them had anticipated this, half were unsure what precautions to take.The most prevalent detection strategies are distinctive textual style patterns and unusually fast completion times.Overall, survey responses show that the research community is aware of the problem and taking measures, but existing efforts remain insufficient to fully address it.Finally, we derive a set of considerations to guide future crowdsourced free-text data collection in the era of LLMs.We make the survey and an anonymized version of the responses available for researchers 1 .
